y reward models in standard tasks."}, "statement": "RLHF can be formulated under a general preference oracle without assuming a traditional reward function or Bradley-Terry model.", "support": [{"evidence": "source-a9ab68e18d3c4eb6", "explanation": "The authors explicitly define their framing around general preference oracles instead of traditional reward functions.", "passage": "In particular, we do not assume the existence of a reward function and an oracle preference signal drawn from the Bradley-Terry model as most of the prior works do."}]}], "evidence": ["source-2d0bdfbb176d43bb", "source-a9ab68e18d3c4eb6"], "findings": "RLHF can be formulated under a general preference oracle without assuming a traditional reward function or Bradley-Terry model. [source-a9ab68e18d3c4eb6]", "id": "notebook-proj-emergent-prefs-01-v1", "limitations": "Empirical validation remains limited to specific algorithmic configurations; long-term longitudinal path dependence of preferences without explicit instruction has not been fully isolated.", "next_questions": "How do preference oracles evolve over extended trajectories of multi-turn agent interaction?", "project": "proj-emergent-prefs-01", "reason": "Synthesizing collected literature on general preference oracles and RLHF formulations for project proj-emergent-prefs-01.", "summary": "Investigation of reverse-KL regularized minimax games between LLMs under general preference oracles, showing how relative feedback frameworks bypass rigid reward functions.", "title": "General Preference Oracles in RLHF without Explicit Reward Functions", "type": "notebook"}], "base_version": 6, "summary": "This cycle evaluates preference formation models in machine learning, specifically focusing on whether RLHF frameworks can be generalized beyond fixed reward functions to capture experience-driven preference oracles.", "title": "Experience-Driven Preference Formation and General Preference Oracles"}, "provider_requests_sent": 1, "result_hash": "6ca3